Let $w(x) = e^{-Q(x)}$ be a Freud-type weight on $\mathbb{R}$, where $Q:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ is an even, convex, $C^2$ function of sufficient growth at infinity (for example, $Q(x)=|x|^{\alpha}$, $\alpha>1$), so that all moments $\int_{\mathbb{R}} x^k w(x)\,dx$ are finite. Let $\{p_n\}_{n\ge 0}$ denote the orthonormal polynomial sequence with respect to $w$, and for each $n\ge 1$ let $x_1^{(n)} < x_2^{(n)} < \cdots < x_n^{(n)}$ be the (real, simple) zeros of $p_n$.

For a configuration $\mathbf{x} = (x_1,\dots,x_n) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ of $n$ distinct points, define the discrete logarithmic energy in the external field $Q$ by
$$E_n(\mathbf{x}) \;=\; -\sum_{1 \le i < j \le n} \log|x_i - x_j| \;+\; \tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n} Q(x_i).$$
It is known that the zero vector $\mathbf{x}^{(n)} = (x_1^{(n)},\dots,x_n^{(n)})$ is a critical point of $E_n$ on $\{x_1<\cdots<x_n\}$.

In the theory of electrostatic models for zeros of classical (Hermite, Laguerre, Jacobi, and related) orthogonal polynomials, one considers additional equilibrium notions beyond being a critical configuration, including:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}$ is a \emph{global minimizer} of $E_n$;
\item[(b)] $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}$ is a \emph{strict local minimizer} of $E_n$ (stable equilibrium with positive definite Hessian);
\item[(c)] each individual coordinate $x_k^{(n)}$ minimizes $E_n$ when the other coordinates are held fixed (a coordinatewise/partial equilibrium condition);
\item[(d)] characterizations of $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}$ as the unique solution of a system of polynomial equations expressing the balance of forces between the movable charges and the external field induced by $Q$.
\end{itemize}

Question: For Freud-type orthogonal polynomials with weight $w=e^{-Q}$ as above, does the zero configuration $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}$ satisfy any of the equilibrium properties (a)–(d) (or other analogous equilibrium notions known for the classical electrostatic models), in addition to being a critical point of $E_n$? In particular, determine whether $\mathbf{x}^{(n)}$ is a (local or global) minimum of $E_n$, and identify which classical electrostatic equilibrium characterizations extend to the Freud-type setting.

